{
 "note": "Cavendish Crescent North read off the enumerators' pages for 1881 and 1891, transcribed by A. Hagues and checked by him line by line before import.\n\n**The record held nothing whatever for this street in 1881.** All six households on the enumerator's round - schedules 106 to 111, some forty-five people - are new. They run from Carisbrooke House under John G Woodward, through Alfred Goater the printer at Cavendish Lodge employing three hundred hands, James B Walker and Thomas Bayley, to John W Lewis the iron merchant at Hardwicke House, a Shetlander from Lerwick.\n\n**Four entries the record does hold for 1891 are single people standing for whole households.** Haddon House held only a 'Percy Smith', where the schedule gives seven including the lace manufacturer John Smith, his son, his daughter-in-law and a nine-month-old grandson. Hardwicke House held a '? Finley' with no forename: he is **John Finley Lewis**, a middle name mistaken for a surname, and the other five of his widowed mother's household were missing. The remaining two, at 17 and 10 Cavendish Crescent North, are fragments of the same kind.\n\n**A surname that would not read has been settled.** The two unmarried lace manufacturers at Carisbrooke House in 1891, brothers of 36 and 34, were set down on the working sheet as a placeholder. They are **Philps**.\n\n**Three servants were misread and are corrected here**: Harriett Leech, not Leush; Dinah Swaine, not Omah; Clara Lofty, not Softy. Thomas Bayley's household at schedule 109 is larger than first read - ten people, with four servants rather than one, among them a nursemaid of nineteen from Walberswick in Suffolk.\n\n**One household on this walk is not on this street.** Schedule 7 of 1891, Robert Wilkinson Smith's, is 1 Pelham Crescent, and is filed there rather than on the crescent the enumerator was working along.\n\n**Three 1881 households carry no house number** - schedules 108, 109 and 110 - and are left unfiled rather than guessed at from their place in the walk. Schedule 110, Thomas Walker the seed crusher's, heads the street 'Road North' and may not be on this crescent at all.",
 "source": "1881 and 1891 census enumerators' pages for Cavendish Crescent North, Nottingham, transcribed by A. Hagues.",
